ADDRESS: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer
==================================================
John Henry Beyer (February 13, 1933 – January 20, 2026) was an American architect. He was a founding partner of  [LINK: (Beyer Blinder Belle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beyer_Blinder_Belle]  and an advocate for  [LINK: (historic preservation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historic_preservation]  of the  [LINK: (architecture of New York City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture_of_New_York_City] .


# EARLY LIFE AND EDUCATION #

Born in  [LINK: (Hackensack, New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackensack,_New_Jersey] , on February 13, 1933, to Henry and Mildred Beyer, he was raised in  [LINK: (Teaneck, New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teaneck,_New_Jersey] ; his father was a jeweler and his mother a homemaker. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]  He earned an undergraduate degree from  [LINK: (Denison University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denison_University] , majoring in music and sculpture, and then earned both a bachelor's degree and master's degree from the  [LINK: (Harvard Graduate School of Design) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Graduate_School_of_Design] , where he majored in architecture. [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-ANobit-2]


# CAREER #

Hired as an employee of shopping mall architect  [LINK: (Victor Gruen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Gruen]  after graduating from Harvard, Beyer met his future partners John Belle and  [LINK: (Richard Lewis Blinder) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Lewis_Blinder]  and together founded an architectural firm in 1968 based in  [LINK: (Greenwich Village) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Village]  dedicated to many of the principles that  [LINK: (Jane Jacobs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane_Jacobs]  posited in her book  [LINK: (The Death and Life of Great American Cities) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Death_and_Life_of_Great_American_Cities] , in which she proposed that urban communities should maintain their character, rather than be demolished and replaced by high-rise buildings as part of  [LINK: (urban renewal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_renewal]  projects. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]

In August 1978, he and his firm were hired by the Township Council of  [LINK: (Teaneck, New Jersey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teaneck,_New_Jersey] , his hometown, to develop plans for an $837,000 redesign of the Teaneck Public Library funded by a grant from the United States government. [LINK: ([3]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-3]

The firm was retained by the  [LINK: (Metropolitan Transportation Authority) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Transportation_Authority]  to plan a $113.8 million restoration of  [LINK: (Grand Central Terminal) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Central_Terminal]  that would remove much of the grime that had covered the building over a span of decades since its opening in 1913. [LINK: ([4]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-4]  The project included the addition of a new staircase and the thorough cleaning of the ceiling in the main concourse to make the once-hidden painted skyscape and constellations visible, helping turning it into one of the city's most visited attractions. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]  After the  [LINK: (September 11 terrorist attacks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/September_11_terrorist_attacks] , which Beyer experienced a block away when the first tower was struck, the firm was selected to develop a master plan for the rebuilt  [LINK: (World Trade Center) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Trade_Center_(2001%E2%80%93present)]  that ended up largely being implemented as Beyer and his team had initially envisioned. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]

He was elected in 1979 as a  [LINK: (Fellow of the American Institute of Architects) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fellow_of_the_American_Institute_of_Architects] . [LINK: ([2]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-ANobit-2]


# PERSONAL LIFE AND DEATH #

Beyer married the former Virginia Van Horn in December 1958; [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-5]  the marriage ended in divorce. He and his second wife, Wendy Beyer, were married in 1983 and had three children. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]

Beyer died at his home in  [LINK: (Manhattan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan]  on January 20, 2026, at the age of 92. His wife, Wendy, had died ten days earlier. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_H._Beyer#cite_note-NYTobit-1]


